  "query_preview": "..Isolated thunderstorms have begun to form over the\nhigher terrain of Santa Cruz and Cochise Counties. GOES total\nprecipitable water shows values ranging from 1.1\"-1.3\" across \nthe CWA. Aloft a broad area of high pressure extends from Sonora\nto the Gulf of Mexico and a couple shortwaves are moving \nthrough the Rockies. This will keep Arizona in southerly flow in\nthe mid-levels over the next couple days keeping PWAT values \nabout what they are right now. \n\nToday hi-res guidance shows the current activity continuing into\nthe evening expanding some in coverage over Santa Cruz and\nCochise Counties as well as the White Mountains. Tucson will \nlikely be the northern extent of coverage if storms make it that\nfar. The main threat will be strong outflow winds in excess of \n40 mph with some storms capable of brief heavy rainfall. The \n13/12Z HREF shows a 50-70 percent chance chance of 40-km \nneighborhood probabilities in excess of 30 knots from Tucson \nsouth and eastward. Overnight models also show an outflow from\nstorms in Sonora moving through southeast Arizona. This will at\nleast raise dewpoints a few degrees and could possible lead to \nsome overnight development, but the 13/18Z HRRR has backed off \nthis scenario. \n\nSunday shower and thunderstorm chances expand further north and \nwest into eastern Tohono O'odham Nation and the Tucson metro. \nThe HREF is hinting that storms will be bit stronger tomorrow \ncompared to today with a 40 km neighborhood probability of 10 \npercent for winds in excess of 50 kts from northern Santa Cruz \nCounty to Pinal County during the evening. If storms do make it \ninto Pinal County blowing dust could become an issue on I-10. \nAgain the main threat for storms will be strong outflow winds \nwith locally heavy rainfall. Monday will see similar storm \nchances from Tucson south and eastward as well as the White \nMountains with the same threats as today and Sunday. \n\nAfter Monday a large trough dominates the eastern two thirds of\nthe U.S. and the high moves south and west as a result. This\nwill begin to put Arizona in westerly flow aloft, which\ndecreases PWAT values by about 0.25\" of an inch. At the same \ntime storms in Sonora will also likely be sending outflows into \nsouthern Arizona. This will be enough to continue slight chance\nPoPs in Santa Cruz County and southern Cochise County each \nafternoon through at least Thursday.\n\nWinds will be breezy each afternoon in the Gila River Valley\nwith speeds 12-20 mph and gusts to 30 mph. It looks like these\nwinds increase some next week Wednesday and Thursday and could\nlead to elevated fire weather conditions. Temperatures will be\naround normal the next 7 days.",
